L000527	\({O}(N_s^{2})\) coefficients, which are the only ones that contribute to Heisenberg scaling.
L000528	\subsection{Asymptotic form of \texorpdfstring{$F^{\bm{\mu}}$}{F-bar-mu} in Eq.~\eqref{eq:AsymFmu}}
L000529	The FIM contribution from the derivatives of the mean homodyne signal is given by
L000530	\begin{equation}
L000531	F^{\bm{\mu}}_{ij} =(\partial_i\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}})^{\mathsf{T}}\,\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}^{-1}\,(\partial_j\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}}),
L000532	\end{equation}
L000533	and depends on both the coherent displacement and the squeezing through \(\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}}\) and \(\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}\). In the same conditions~\eqref{Eq:LOtuning} and for \(\phi_3=\pi/4+k_3/N_s\) by substituting the parameter derivatives of the mean $\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}}$~\eqref{EqA:mu} and $\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}^{-1}$ into $F^{\bm{\mu}}$ yields, to the leading order,
L000534	\begin{equation}
L000535	F^{\bm{\mu}}
L000536	=
L000537	N_sN_c\,\mathcal{F}^{\bm{\mu}}(k_1,k_2,k_3,\phi_1)
L000538	\;+\;{O}(N)
L000539	\label{eq:FmuAsym_compact}
L000540	\end{equation}
L000541	where the coefficient matrix is independent of \(N\). Explicitly,
L000542	\begin{equation}
L000543	\mathcal{F}^{\bm{\mu}}
L000544	=
L000545	\frac{2}{\Lambda}\,
L000546	\begin{pmatrix}
L000547	0&0&0&0\\
L000548	0&D_2&0&0\\
L000549	0&0&0&0\\
L000550	0&0&0&0
L000551	\end{pmatrix},
L000552	\label{eq:Fmu2_matrix}
L000553	\end{equation}
L000554	with
L000555	\begin{equation}
L000556	D_2=1+2k_1^{2}+2k_2^{2}+4k_3^{2}+2(k_1+k_2)\big[(k_1-k_2)\cos(\phi_1)+2k_3\sin(\phi_1)\big].
L000557	\end{equation}
L000558	We find that the only entry with a leading
L000559	\({O}(N_sN_c)={O}(N^2)\) contribution is the $[F^{\bm{\mu}}]_{22}$ component, while all other entries are at most \({O}(N)\) or \({O}(N^0)\), since \(N_{s,c}={O}(N)\).
