L000642	\label{eq:Nonsingular_k}
L000643	\end{equation}
L000644	At these points, the leading ${O}(N^2)$ Fisher information loses rank, so the four-parameter model is locally unidentifiable at Heisenberg scaling, and at least one parameter combination can only be resolved from subleading terms.
L000645	\section{Maximum-Likelihood Estimation}\label{Appendix C}
L000646	Here, we derive the maximum-likelihood estimators (MLEs) for the Gaussian statistics generated by two-port homodyne detection. Repeating the experiment \(M\) times yields an i.i.d.\ data set \(\bm{x}_1,\ldots,\bm{x}_M\), where each \(\bm{x}_m=(x_{1,m},x_{2,m})^{\mathsf T}\) is distributed according to \(p(\bm{x}\,|\,\bm{\phi})\) in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Gaussian_pdf_main}, with mean \(\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}}\) and covariance \(\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}\).
L000647	\begin{figure}
L000648	    \centering
L000649	    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{CRB_Bias_M_combined.png}
L000650	    \caption{Asymptotic performance of the MLEs for a total mean photon number $N=10$ with equal resource split, $N_c = N_s = 5$. The different parameter values ($\phi_0, \phi_1, \phi_2$) in the figure assess the robustness of the estimation to changes in the true parameter values. Top row: the normalized CRB $\Delta\widetilde{\phi}_i/(\Delta\phi_i)^{\mathrm{CRB}}$ versus the number of experimental iterations $M$ for $i=0,1,2,3$. The convergence of all curves to unity shows that the MLE saturates the CRB in the large-$M$ limit. Bottom row: the unbiasedness ratio $\mathrm{E}[\widetilde{\phi}_i]/\phi_i$ as a function of $M$ for the same settings, which converges towards 1 as the estimator bias becomes negligible, typically for sample sizes of order $M\approx100$. The beam-splitter angle is set to the balanced working point $k_3=0$ ($\phi_3=\pi/4$), and the LO constants are fixed to $k_1=k_2=0.5$.}
L000651	    \label{fig:CRB_Bias_M_combined}
L000652	\end{figure}
L000653	The likelihood function for \(\bm{\phi}\) is given by
L000654	\begin{equation}
L000655	\mathcal{L}(\bm{\phi}\,|\,\bm{x}_1,\ldots,\bm{x}_M)
L000656	=\prod_{m=1}^{M} p(\bm{x}_m\,|\,\bm{\phi}),
L000657	\label{eq:App_Likelihood}
L000658	\end{equation}
L000659	and the MLE \(\widetilde{\bm{\phi}}_{\mathrm{MLE}}\) maximizes \(\mathcal{L}\), equivalently the log-likelihood \(\ell(\bm{\phi})=\log\mathcal{L}=\sum_{m=1}^{M}\log p(\bm{x}_m\,|\,\bm{\phi})\).
L000660	The estimator is obtained from the condition
L000661	\begin{equation}
L000662	\nabla_{\bm{\phi}}\ell(\bm{\phi})\Big|_{\bm{\phi}=\widetilde{\bm{\phi}}_{\mathrm{MLE}}}
L000663	=\bm{0}.
L000664	\label{eq:App_score_condition}
L000665	\end{equation}
L000666	Using \(\nabla_{\bm{\phi}}\log(\det\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}})=\mathrm{Tr}\!\left(\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}^{-1}\nabla_{\bm{\phi}}\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}\right)\) and rewriting the quadratic form in trace notation,
L000667	\[
L000668	(\bm{x}-\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}})^{\mathsf T}\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}^{-1}(\bm{x}-\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}})
L000669	=
L000670	\mathrm{Tr}\!\left[\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}^{-1}(\bm{x}-\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}})(\bm{x}-\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}})^{\mathsf T}\right],
L000671	\]
L000672	one arrives at the coupled MLE equations in the compact form
L000673	\begin{align}
L000674	\bm{0}
L000675	&=
L000676	\big(\nabla_{\bm{\phi}}\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}}\big)^{\mathsf T}\,
L000677	\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}^{-1}
L000678	\left(\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}}-\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\bm{x}_m
L000679	\right)
L000680	+\frac{1}{2}\,
L000681	\mathrm{Tr}\!\left[
L000682	\big(\nabla_{\bm{\phi}}\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}^{-1}\big)
L000683	\left(\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}
L000684	-\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}
L000685	(\bm{x}_m-\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}})
L000686	(\bm{x}_m-\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}})^{\mathsf T}
L000687	\right)
L000688	\right]
L000689	\;\;\Bigg|_{\bm{\phi}=\widetilde{\bm{\phi}}_{\mathrm{MLE}}}.
L000690	\label{eq:App_score_final}
L000691	\end{align}
L000692	Equation~\eqref{eq:App_score_final} is the standard score condition for a multivariate Gaussian model. In general, it does not admit a closed-form solution. In the main text, we solve it numerically and use the resulting MLE to verify the convergence of CRBs (Figs.~\ref{fig:CRB_vs_M} and~\ref{fig:CRB_vs_N} in the main text).
L000693	
L000694	A useful simplification follows from the structure of the homodyne output statistics: in our scheme $\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}$ is independent of the phase $\phi_1$. Hence $\partial_{\phi_1}\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}=\bm{0}$, so the covariance-dependent trace term in Eq.~\eqref{eq:App_score_final} vanishes identically in the $\phi_1$ component. The MLE for $\phi_1$ therefore reduces to,
L000695	\begin{equation}
L000696	\left[(\partial_{\phi_1}\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}})^{\mathsf T}\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}^{-1}
L000697	\left(\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}}-\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\bm{x}_m
L000698	\right)\right]_{\phi_1=\widetilde{\phi}_{1\mathrm{MLE}}}
L000699	=0,
L000700	\label{eq:App_phi_ML}
L000701	\end{equation}
L000702	where \(\widetilde{\bm{\mu}}_{\bm{\phi}}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\bm{x}_m\) is the estimator of  the sample mean \(\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}}\). This makes explicit that the estimator \(\widetilde{\phi}_1\) is determined solely by the mean homodyne response. By contrast, the remaining parameters \(\phi_0\), \(\phi_2\), and \(\phi_3\) enter both \(\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}}\) and \(\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}\), and their MLEs follow from the equation \eqref{eq:App_score_final}. Figure~\ref{fig:CRB_Bias_M_combined} illustrates the asymptotic performance of the MLEs for different choices of the true parameter values. The results show that the estimators converge to the CRB and become effectively unbiased as the number of experimental iterations $M$ increases, indicating that the estimation scheme remains robust under variations of the underlying parameters.
