L000704	\section{Effect of homodyne inefficiency}\label{Appendix D}
L000705	In this section, we quantify how finite homodyne inefficiency affects the FIM and corresponding CRBs. Our scheme saturates the CRBs simultaneously already for modest photon numbers, $N=5$--$10$ (see Fig.~\ref{fig:CRB_vs_N} in the main text). We identify the finite-photon-number regime in which the Heisenberg-scaling behavior remains visible for finite homodyne efficiencies.
L000706	
L000707	In Fig.~\ref{fig:lossy_homodyne_model}, we model imperfect homodyne detection by a beam splitter of transmittance $\eta\in(0,1]$, preceding each ideal detector, which mixes the output modes with vacuum before detection~\cite{PhysRevA.96.062304}. For the two-port homodyne with equal efficiency $\eta$, the measured quadrature operator becomes $\hat{\bm{x}}_{\bm{\theta},\eta}=\sqrt{\eta}\,\hat{\bm{x}}_{\bm \theta} +\sqrt{1-\eta}\,\hat{\bm x}_{\rm vac}$, where $\hat{\bm x}_{\rm vac}$ has covariance $\mathbb I_2/2$, where $\mathbb{I}_2$ is $2\times2$ identity matrix. The joint two-port homodyne statistics remain Gaussian with~\cite{PhysRevA.96.062304, PRXQuantum.3.010202}
L000708	\begin{equation}
L000709	\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}}^{\eta}
L000710	=
L000711	\sqrt{\eta}\,
L000712	\bm{\mu}_{\bm{\phi}},
L000713	\qquad
L000714	\Sigma_{\bm{\phi}}^{\eta}
L000715	=
L000716	\eta\,\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\phi}}
L000717	+\frac{1-\eta}{2}\,\mathbb{I}_2.
L000718	\label{eq:lossy_moments}
L000719	\end{equation}
L000720	\begin{figure}
L000721	    \centering
L000722	    \includegraphics[width=0.6\linewidth]{Lossy_HD.png}
L000723	    \caption{Detector inefficiency at each output port is modeled by a fictitious beam splitter of transmittance \(\eta\in(0,1]\), placed before the ideal balanced homodyne detectors at both output ports.}
L000724	    \label{fig:lossy_homodyne_model}
L000725	\end{figure}
L000726	Since $\partial_i\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\eta}=\sqrt{\eta}\,\partial_i\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\boldsymbol{\phi}}$ and $ \partial_i\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\phi}}^{\eta}=\eta\,\partial_i\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\phi}}$, from Eq.~\eqref{eq:FIMGaussian_main} in the main text, the inefficient-homodyne FIM can be written as
L000727	\begin{equation}
L000728	F_{ij}^{\eta}
L000729	=
L000730	\frac{\eta^2}{2}
